From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0251] feat(apps): bounded submission queue in front of the apps RSC

C3-05 is a storage change and nothing else.  The apps array has always run one
transaction at a time, and a display-corner request is itself up to five flights
(guard, guard, then three supplies).  Until now the owner answered every second
caller with a refusal; from here the calls that used to be refused wait, in
arrival order, until the previous transaction retires.  What does not change is
the acceptance rule this card is judged by: with one request outstanding, the
existing rows and the TCS command sequence are what 0147 sent.

The queue.  SweetAppsQueue.h/.cpp is fixed storage and no policy: eight slots, a
head and a count, counters for accepted/dispatched/high-water, and a sequence
number stamped at push.  It allocates nothing, takes no lock, prints nothing and
calls nothing in IOKit; the owner serializes every call from its gate and decides
what a refusal looks like.  push() copies the record in and refuses when full
instead of dropping the oldest, because two accepted votes for one resource have
no defined order once they collapse; the refusal does not stamp a sequence.  The
record is self-contained by necessity: the caller's stack and its live objects
are gone by the time a waiting request is dispatched, so the kind, the owner's
client slot, the resource tag, the payload and the token issued before the wait
all travel inside it.  find() answers the one question the duplicate rule needs -
same kind, same client, same resource - and waiting() answers whether a client
still has anything parked, which is what detach has to ask.

Admission is split in two.  validate() is the pre-admission screen: it takes a
proposal that has not been stored anywhere, checks it against the same tables and
the same committed ledger the launch uses, and refuses it before any token
exists, so a refused submission leaves the caller's previous completion and its
token sequence exactly as they were, with no writes, no status slot touched and
no queue entry.  It is deliberately a second reading of the same rules rather
than a call into launch: the launch needs a record to fail against, and a screen
that first had to publish a token would turn a typo into a lost completion.  What
the screen cannot decide is the state that may move while a request waits - the
TCS, the executor door, the mode, the poison - so dispatch evaluates those again
and reports them against the token the queued request already holds.  A direct
(non-queued) dispatch publishes no status on failure and writes no Pending: the
caller learns from the return value, which is what it got before this card.

Dispatch and retirement.  admit() still owns the order of the checks it had -
mode, poison, executor, accepting, inactive, kind - and then walks: screen,
duplicate, capacity, token.  If nothing is in flight the request becomes the
current one with seq 0 and is launched at once; otherwise the token is issued,
the caller's slot is marked Pending so the queued request is queryable while it
waits, and the record is pushed.  retireFlight() drives the queue: while a
request is in flight nothing starts, and every waiting request is launched in
sequence order by the same path a direct one took.  A queued request that fails
at dispatch is marked Failed against its own token and does not stop the rest.
The isolation state is a hard stop in both directions: dispatchNext() refuses to
start anything once the owner is poisoned and drains the waiters with the
isolation reason instead of running them behind a poisoned transaction, so the
card's "A fails, B must not cross" holds even when B arrived before the failure.

Panel votes are not a queue kind.  A fenced panel vote arms a real reset-low
window around a supply that the display needs; letting it wait would mean either
holding the fence across a stranger's transaction or releasing it before the
supply is back.  A panel vote that arrives while anything is in flight - current
or waiting - is refused with its own row, and the ownership of that short window
is the subject of C3-06 instead of being quietly widened here.

The diagnostic.  One row set marks every decision: the queue self-test, the
dispatch with its wait mode, the refusals with their reason, and the drain.  The
self-test is off unless the tree says otherwise: property sweet,rpmh-queue-selftest
exactly four bytes of 1, at most six attempts, hooked on the first retirement
because no rail is owned before then.  It submits two legal rail raises through
the ordinary entry point from two different client slots, so it exercises the
queue path it claims to exercise - the second raise can only be admitted because
the first is in flight - and it leaves that effect visible: both rails stay
raised for the boot.  Withdrawal is C3-06's, and the demo says so in its row.

One status slot per client is a caveat, not a defect.  A client whose request is
waiting has its newer token and its older completion cannot be queried any more
(NotFound); the same is true of every direct request today when a second one
arrives.  Queued requests are therefore documented as queryable by the token
they were given, in arrival order per client, and nothing else is promised.

The tree.  mk-mh2lm-mdss-adt.py learns one flag, --rpmh-queue-selftest, which
appends a 4-byte property to the display owner node - the node the driver reads
it from, which is where the queue lives - and refuses to emit it without that
node, so a tree cannot carry a switch nobody consumes.  It is the only difference
between this round's candidate and the C3-04 one, and the tests prove the removal
is exact.

Tests.  test-mh2lm-apps-queue.py is the unit on its own - capacity, the refusal
without a stamp, arrival order across the array wrap, exact-resource lookup, the
payload and pre-issued token surviving the caller, and the boot-time reset - and
also compiles the unit without the board macro to prove it is empty there.  The
resources fixture gains the driver-level contracts: one TCS owner across a
cross-client arrival, an explicit queue-full refusal that spends no token and
leaves the previous completion readable, and a waiter refused at the isolation
state with its rail never programmed.  The other fixtures move to the new
ordering where they asserted the old "second caller is Busy" outcome: a request
that cannot replace a pending transaction is now the resource already in flight
(duplicate) or a fenced panel vote, and the harness's acknowledgement helper
learns that retiring a flight may start a waiting one in the same window, so it
asserts that the queue shrinks and that nothing waits without an owner instead of
asserting that the machine is idle.
diff --git a/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetAppsQueue.cpp b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetAppsQueue.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2f5fa9b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetAppsQueue.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,74 @@
+/* C3-05: fixed-capacity FIFO, no allocation and no policy.
+ *
+ * pop() only moves the head forward; the slot is overwritten by the next
+ * push(), so a retired request's bytes are not retained past the owner's
+ * dispatch. Nothing here calls into IOKit, locks, or the log: the owner
+ * serializes every call from its gate, and a refusal is the owner's row to
+ * print, not this unit's.
+ */
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MH2LM)
+#include "SweetAppsQueue.h"
+
+SweetAppsQueue::SweetAppsQueue()
+{
+	reset();
+}
+
+void
+SweetAppsQueue::reset(void)
+{
+	for (unsigned i = 0; i < kSweetAppsQueueCapacity; ++i) fSlots[i] = SweetAppsRequest();
+	fHead = fCount = fNextSeq = 0;
+	fAccepted = fDispatched = fHighWater = 0;
+}
+
+bool
+SweetAppsQueue::push(const SweetAppsRequest &request, SweetAppsRequest *stored)
+{
+	if (full()) return false;
+	unsigned tail = (fHead + fCount) % kSweetAppsQueueCapacity;
+	fSlots[tail] = request;
+	fSlots[tail].seq = ++fNextSeq;
+	fCount++;
+	fAccepted++;
+	if (fCount > fHighWater) fHighWater = fCount;
+	if (stored) *stored = fSlots[tail];
+	return true;
+}
+
+const SweetAppsRequest *
+SweetAppsQueue::head(void) const
+{
+	if (!fCount) return NULL;
+	return &fSlots[fHead];
+}
+
+void
+SweetAppsQueue::pop(void)
+{
+	if (!fCount) return;
+	fHead = (fHead + 1) % kSweetAppsQueueCapacity;
+	fCount--;
+	fDispatched++;
+}
+
+const SweetAppsRequest *
+SweetAppsQueue::find(unsigned kind, unsigned client, unsigned rail) const
+{
+	for (unsigned i = 0; i < fCount; ++i) {
+		const SweetAppsRequest &slot = fSlots[(fHead + i) % kSweetAppsQueueCapacity];
+		if (slot.kind == kind && slot.client == client && slot.rail == rail) return &slot;
+	}
+	return NULL;
+}
+
+bool
+SweetAppsQueue::waiting(unsigned client) const
+{
+	for (unsigned i = 0; i < fCount; ++i) {
+		const SweetAppsRequest &slot = fSlots[(fHead + i) % kSweetAppsQueueCapacity];
+		if (slot.client == client) return true;
+	}
+	return false;
+}
+#endif
\ No newline at end of file
diff --git a/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetAppsQueue.h b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetAppsQueue.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..90dc811
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dxb/xnu/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetAppsQueue.h
@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+/* C3-05: the bounded submission queue in front of the apps RSC.
+ *
+ * The array runs one transaction at a time, and a display-corner request is
+ * itself up to five flights (guard, guard, then three supplies). Callers that
+ * arrive while one transaction is in flight used to be refused outright; they
+ * now wait here, in arrival order, until the owner retires the previous one.
+ *
+ * This unit owns no policy and touches no hardware. It stores a record the
+ * owner filled in, hands back the same bytes, and runs out of room explicitly.
+ * There is no dynamic storage, no timer, no reference counting, no coalescing
+ * and no drop-oldest: what it accepts it still holds until the owner retires
+ * it. The caller's stack and its live objects are gone by the time a queued
+ * request is dispatched, so everything needed to rebuild it must already be
+ * inside the record.
+ */
+#ifndef SWEET_APPS_QUEUE_H
+#define SWEET_APPS_QUEUE_H
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_MH2LM)
+#include <IOKit/IOTypes.h>
+
+/* Eight is one waiting request per client slot. A request larger than that is
+ * refused, never merged into an older one: two waiting votes for the same
+ * resource have no defined order once they collapse. */
+enum : unsigned { kSweetAppsQueueCapacity = 8 };
+
+struct SweetAppsRequest {
+	unsigned kind;		/* owner-defined tag */
+	unsigned client;	/* owner's client slot, not a caller pointer */
+	unsigned rail;		/* owner's resource tag; kinds without a
+				 * sub-target use 0 */
+	uint64_t param[2];	/* owner's payload, decoded by the owner */
+	uint64_t token;		/* issued before enqueueing, so the caller can
+				 * already query the request it submitted */
+	unsigned seq;		/* stamped by push(); never reused in a boot */
+};
+
+class SweetAppsQueue {
+	SweetAppsRequest fSlots[kSweetAppsQueueCapacity];
+	unsigned fHead, fCount, fNextSeq;
+	unsigned fAccepted, fDispatched, fHighWater;
+public:
+	SweetAppsQueue();
+	void reset(void);
+	bool empty(void) const { return fCount == 0; }
+	bool full(void) const { return fCount == kSweetAppsQueueCapacity; }
+	unsigned depth(void) const { return fCount; }
+	unsigned accepted(void) const { return fAccepted; }
+	unsigned dispatched(void) const { return fDispatched; }
+	unsigned highWater(void) const { return fHighWater; }
+	/* Copy in, never overwrite: false means the queue is full and the caller
+	 * must refuse the request - dropping an older one would silently reorder
+	 * two accepted votes. full() answers the same question before a token is
+	 * spent. The stamped sequence is what an ordering claim about a ring row
+	 * is checked against. */
+	bool push(const SweetAppsRequest &request, SweetAppsRequest *stored = NULL);
+	/* Oldest request, still owned by the queue. NULL when empty. */
+	const SweetAppsRequest *head(void) const;
+	/* Retire the head after it has been dispatched or failed. */
+	void pop(void);
+	/* One waiting request for exactly this resource, or NULL. Same client and
+	 * same resource means two requests would drive the same field once
+	 * dispatched, so the owner refuses the second one instead of queueing it. */
+	const SweetAppsRequest *find(unsigned kind, unsigned client, unsigned rail) const;
+	/* Whether this client still has a waiting request, without naming one. */
+	bool waiting(unsigned client) const;
+};
+#endif
+#endif
